{:edb/db-before-t 280 :edb/db-after-t 281 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533593 50 #inst "2026-10-11T10:57:09.366Z" 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054628 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410421 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410425 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410427 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186064308 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410419 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186068786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410416 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186078563 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410432 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186083226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410438 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186105737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410422 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186105819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410420 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186115219 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410434 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186115317 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410433 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186120471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118940 13194139533593 17592186045417 false] [17592186120471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410415 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186120471 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186120471 1071 "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\n- $5,\\,10,\\,15,\\,…$\n- ${a}_{n+1}=6{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=18,\\,n\\ge 1$\n- $f(n+1)=2f(n)+3,\\,f(1)=1,\\,n\\ge 1$" 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186120471 1071 "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\n1. $5, 10, 15, …$\n2. $a_{n + 1} = 6a_{n}, a_{1} = 18, n \\ge 1$\n3. $f(n + 1) = 2f(n) + 3, f(1) = 1, n \\ge 1$" 13194139533593 17592186045417 false] [17592186120471 1074 "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)}=\\frac{10}{5} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{f(3)}{f(2)}=\\frac{15}{10} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is a geometric sequence. The recursive rule ${a}_{n+1}=6{a}_{n}$ tells us that each term is $6$ times the previous term, so the common ratio is $r=6.$\n\n- Sequence III is not a geometric sequence. To see this, we can calculate the first $3$ terms of the sequence and then compute the ratios of consecutive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =1 \\\\ f(2) & =2\\cdot 1+3=5 \\\\ f(3) & =2\\cdot 5+3=13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first two pairs of consecutive terms have different ratios:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)}=\\frac{5}{1} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{f(3)}{f(2)}=\\frac{13}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence II is a geometric sequence." 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186129146 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410418 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186162095 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410431 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186162108 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410430 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186162154 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410436 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186162159 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410429 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186166738 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410437 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186166969 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410428 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186176042 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410426 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186176063 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410435 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186184140 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410424 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186184248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410417 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186198716 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194922 13194139533593 17592186045417 false] [17592186198716 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410381 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186198716 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186198716 1071 "Let $I = [0, 2]⊂R$ and $X_{i} = {i} \\cdot [0, i]$. Which of the following represents $⋃_(i ∈ I)X_{i}$?" 13194139533593 17592186045417 false] [17592186198716 1071 "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186198716 1074 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}={1}\\times [0,1]={(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/71/71dd97607df48caf579c2e476520507ede035f65c336064f022cd900b6c120fc.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,i]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](images/4b/4b87c830620f54e5b3a3bb7923be971b4d9d4abb98cfb3dcdc8387f376a0b452.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/6b/6b5b39546eabacdf948bd87e793727fdc8cd8f2664df037be53bfb630fc41670.png)" 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186208845 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410423 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186233094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410378 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186233427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410394 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186233427 1071 "![](images/bc/bc3809e55e601738d56be6885475ed442dec1fb3a0d2e19da14be045510f73dc.png)\n\nThe above scatter plot shows the relationship between age and years of experience of a group of professional soccer players. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Only one player has exactly $10$ years of experience.\n- There are two players aged $21.$\n- None of the players has fewer than $6$ years of experience." 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186233427 1071 "![](images/bc/bc3809e55e601738d56be6885475ed442dec1fb3a0d2e19da14be045510f73dc.png)\n\nThe above scatter plot shows the relationship between age and years of experience of a group of professional soccer players. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - Only one player has exactly $10$ years of experience.\nII. - There are two players aged $21.$\nIII. - None of the players has fewer than $6$ years of experience." 13194139533593 17592186045417 false] [17592186233427 1074 "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $10,$ namely, $(21,10).$ This means only one of the players has exactly $10$ years of experience.\n\n![](images/20/209759f5d70fd8587740d1da840f9da838a3c9a055a0d1e962f76c1cd6341fd3.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The scatter plot has two dots whose horizontal coordinate equals $21,$ namely, $(21,10)$ and $(21,12).$ This means that two players are $21$ years old.\n\n![](images/75/753c503d6751363b1c7d2dc449f1c5f11baec159b1e938e8c807938512111750.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. From the scatter plot, one player has fewer than $6$ years of experience. The dot below the horizontal line represents this player.\n\n![](images/66/66facd1a0683f420aa2ac3d7bfd54290bdb009850bc66cc7523ee569ec81daea.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186233427 1074 "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $10,$ namely, $(21,10).$ This means only one of the players has exactly $10$ years of experience.\n\n![](images/20/209759f5d70fd8587740d1da840f9da838a3c9a055a0d1e962f76c1cd6341fd3.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The scatter plot has two dots whose horizontal coordinate equals $21,$ namely, $(21,10)$ and $(21,12).$ This means that two players are $21$ years old.\n\n- Statement III is false. From the scatter plot, one player has fewer than $6$ years of experience. The dot below the horizontal line represents this player.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533593 17592186045417 false] [17592186233458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410407 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186233494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410382 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186233494 1074 "The given points are ${{(7,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})},$ ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(2,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}\\,.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (2,1) & =(\\frac{7+a}{2},\\frac{-4+b}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2=\\frac{7+a}{2} \\\\ 1=\\frac{-4+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\Rightarrow \\,{\\begin{aligned}a=-3 \\\\ b=6.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(-3,6)$ and $a+b=3.$" 13194139533593 17592186045417 false] [17592186233494 1074 "The given points are ${{(7,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})},$ ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(2,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}\\,.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (2,1) & =(\\frac{7+a}{2},\\frac{-4+b}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2=\\frac{7+a}{2} \\\\ 1=\\frac{-4+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}a=-3 \\\\ b=6.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(-3,6)$ and $a+b=3.$" 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186233501 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410384 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186233501 1074 "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(1,\\,0).$   Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{'},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2x & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-(2x+y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x+y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,0)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,0)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2x+y}{x})}_{(1,\\,0)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+0}{1} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is ${m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(1,\\,0)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{'}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}).$" 13194139533593 17592186045417 false] [17592186233501 1074 "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(1,\\,0).$   Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{′},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2x & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-(2x+y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x+y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,0)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,0)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2x+y}{x})}_{(1,\\,0)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+0}{1} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is ${m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(1,\\,0)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}).$" 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186233616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410386 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186233750 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410392 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186234156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410412 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186234235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410393 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186234327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410380 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186329947 1071 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=e.$" 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186329947 1071 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=xln⁡x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=e.$" 13194139533593 17592186045417 false] [17592186329947 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=e$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=e} & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡e \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2.$" 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186329947 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=ln⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+(ln⁡x)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+ln⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=e$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=e} & =1+ln⁡x \\\\  & =1+ln⁡e \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2.$" 13194139533593 17592186045417 false] [17592186389975 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:" 13194139533593 17592186045417 true] [17592186389975 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $